00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
hi there  steve kaufman here uh a little different  today i'm standing in front of a print  here of a cafe i think it's called cafe  le soir which was painted by van gogh or  van gogh or whatever his name is dutch  painter in southern france  it's an image of language takes you to  exotic places and you can mix different  cultures and that's what i love about it  but  today i want to talk about long-term  versus short-term  activities or strategies in language  learning and i thought about it because  i was reflecting on my farsi experience  where i've really been  kind of hitting farsi hard since i came  back from morocco prior to that i've  been working on arabic now i'm doing  sort of three days a week of farsi three  days a week of arabic  but  even with this limited exposure to farsi  i am having conversations with my tutor  and i am speaking and i'm able to  express things and i'll show you that  later on in a couple of weeks or so  as a part of our 90-day  challenge and the sort of daily clinic  that we're going to do  people are saying when will the daily  clinic what time of the day we haven't  decided yet it's a little bit uncertain  we'll have to feel our way to that and i  will be letting you know here once we  get into the 90-day challenge how all of  this is going to work so just stay tuned  at this  channel but  long term versus short term which is  also something to consider as we go into  a 90 day challenge what are we trying to  achieve so if i compare farsi to check  check despite having learned russian and  having a lot of the vocabulary  and uh you know having all kinds of  advantages it was eight months before i  spoke to a tutor and then i struggled  mightily all right whereas with farsi  but i had a lot of words like right now  and check i have 40 50 000 words  according to our statistics at link in  farsi i have less than 1400 words i  believe i just checked  and yet i'm speaking in farsi  so  why well one thing i've done in farsi is  that i have i have these many stories so  i've listened to them 30 40 50 times so  i have quite a good grasp on a more  limited amount of vocabulary  and part of the reason i do that is  because farsi is intrinsically more  difficult than czech because czech is  written in the latin alphabet farsi is  written in the arabic alphabet it's more  difficult to read therefore in farsi  with czech i had more sort of accessible  sort of intermediate  although it was still difficult but it  was easier to get into authentic content  more difficult to do in farsi  plus i'm motivated to speak to all the  farsi speakers  in vancouver whereas there's no check  speakers for me to talk to in vancouver  so i had two different goals  uh  i know  that even though i can speak to my farsi  speaking tutor  is a long way before i really be able to  understand you know uh news podcasts  radio programs and that kind of thing  because to do that in any language  requires a lot of vocabulary i do  eventually want to get there so  the time that i'm spending on improving  my short term ability to speak is time  that's taking away taken away from  building up enough vocabulary to be able  to read newspaper articles on politics  and so forth  in the case of arabic i'm starting to do  more of you know fighting my way through  al jazeera you know online newspaper  articles  but i still do i still go back to my  repetitive listening of many stories  which are tremendously powerful in terms  of building up your ability to speak so  uh if i have a need to speak now i don't  really have a big need to speak to  arabic speak in arabic and in particular  in modern modern standard arabic so that  my goal there is still somewhat more  passive in terms of wanting to build up  enough vocabulary so that i can follow  things on the internet  whereas in farsi my goal is much more  short term just to be able to converse  with people in farsi so i think it's  important that we have a strategy like  what do we want to achieve the ability  to say a few things in the language in  the short term  if that's all you want to achieve then  you put all your emphasis on that if you  want to also build up your ability to  watch movies and  follow  stuff on the internet then you have to  force yourself into some of those sort  of  longer term activities  acquiring more vocabulary you can't  listen i can't listen to these podcasts  20 30 times they're too long  i give myself some exposure to these  podcasts after having in arabic after  having worked through the text but it's  still very very fuzzy for me just as it  was when i was learning czech month  after month after month i was struggling  to understand the material that i was  listening to  and yet slowly slowly slowly you get  better i should also say that in my  repetitive listening to my even my  arabic and my farsi mini stories  every time i listen  i focus on something else i discover  something else i discover a phrase i  knew but i hadn't really focused on it  or a structure and so that it's i still  find it  compelling in a way to  spend some of my time  working on this basic material to get a  better grip on a natural grip on some of  the structures of the language  and combine that in the case of arabic  with pushing my vocabulary forward  whereas in farsi i'm still more or less  focused on  you know the most common verbs of the  language which appear in our 60  mini-story so um that's kind of what i  wanted to say i think all of us have to  decide based on our goals whether we  want to put more time into the sort of  short-term objectives  or invest in the long-term because  eventually we will be able to speak it's  just that we won't be able to say very  much up front if we're focusing on  on building up our vocabulary for the  long term  so there you have it and uh you know  next friday we start the 90-day  challenge so once again people can study  any language they want i will be holding  regular clinics where i will be showing  you what i am doing  my 90-day challenge is a  for the first time two languages  arabic and farsi  but most people will be doing one  language  and i'll be talking about what i'm doing  and  we will decide on a time of day for  those of you  this is for my clinics those of you who  want to join me  i'm not sure how this is going to work  we will find out as we get into it so  thank you for listening and uh yeah  today's  monday thursday will be my last  regular video until we get into our 90  day challenge starting next friday  oh and i almost forgot  um i'm going to fukuoka in the fall for  the polyglot conference there fukuoka  japan  and i was contacted by a lady who worked  for  the canadian broadcasting corporation  uh the cbc  who which has a program called the  nature of things with david suzuki and  they're doing a program on polyglots and  she would like to hear from any  polyglots anyone who speaks two or more  languages  who is prepared to sort of tackle  learning japanese that is to say anyone  who is going to fukuoka i think that's  what she wants or otherwise interested  in working on japanese because she wants  to  somehow interview these people or keep  track of or understand better how these  polyglots would go about learning  japanese in a lead up to the fukuoka  polyglot conference so if you are at all  interested in contacting eileen is her  name i will leave her email address in  the description box here thank you bye  [Music]  you

